Eusebe), used and redistributed under the MIT License. The original copyright
notice and permission notice are preserved in full in the LICENSE file, as the
MIT License requires.

What was inherited from Record-able
-----------------------------------
The following components are ported from Record-able with their behaviour
intentionally unchanged; only the package, the logging category and the branding
were adapted. Each file carries a header naming its origin:

  dev/streamable/audio/AudioCapture.java            (device detection)
  dev/streamable/audio/JavaAudioCapture.java        (Java Sound capture)
  dev/streamable/audio/OpenALAudioCapture.java      (OpenAL capture)
  dev/streamable/audio/OpenALLoopbackCapture.java   (OpenAL loopback game audio)
  dev/streamable/audio/WASAPIAudioCapture.java      (Windows loopback helper)
  dev/streamable/ffmpeg/FfmpegBundleManager.java    (FFmpeg download + verification)
  dev/streamable/ffmpeg/EncoderType.java
  dev/streamable/ffmpeg/MediaCodecEncoder.java
  dev/streamable/recording/VideoMetadata.java       (probing, thumbnails, duration)
  dev/streamable/recording/DiskSpaceGuardian.java
  dev/streamable/util/PlatformUtils.java            (platform detection)

dev/streamable/util/PerformanceMetrics.java keeps Record-able's public API but
was reimplemented without the Micrometer dependency.

The recording architecture - raw frames to FFmpeg over stdin, audio captured in
parallel and muxed afterwards with a measured `-itsoffset` - is Record-able's
design and is preserved deliberately, because it is what keeps long recordings
free of audio drift.

Third-party components
----------------------
* MCEF Modern (https://github.com/DimasKama/mcef-modern) by DimasKama, licensed
  LGPL-2.1. Used as an optional runtime dependency; Stream-able links against
  its published API and ships no modified copy of it.
* JCEF / java-cef and the Chromium Embedded Framework, reached through MCEF
  Modern, under their respective BSD-style licences.
* FFmpeg, invoked as an external process and never bundled. Builds are
  downloaded at the user's request from the upstream mirrors Record-able already
  used, with checksum verification.

Nothing in this project is copied from OBS Studio. OBS is referenced only as
inspiration for workflow and terminology ("browser source", "transform box",
"multistreaming").
